1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Yin-chên''' 胤禎 (H. 破塵居士, Dec. 13, 1678-1735, Oct. 8, third Emperor of the Ch'ing dynasty, ruled in the years 1723-36, under the reign-title '''Yung-chêng''' 雍正. He was the fourth son of [https://www.geni.com/peo
2 CONC ple/Kangxi/6000000019155600599 Emperor Shêng-tsu]. His mother, [https://www.geni.com/people/Xiaogong/6000000009821590180 Empress Hsiao-kung] [q.v.], was a maid-servant in the Palace, but a year after he was born she was elevated t
2 CONC o an imperial consort of the fourth rank. In 1698 he was made a prince of the third rank, and in 1709 was raised to the first rank with the designation, '''Yung''' 雍親王. As a prince, he lived quietly at home, and became well-versed
2 CONC  in Chinese and in Buddhistic literature. But when his brother, the Heir Apparent [https://www.geni.com/people/Yinreng/6000000009822743523 Yin-jêng] [q.v.], twice showed signs of mental unbalance (1708 and 1712) and lost the favor
2 CONC  of [https://www.geni.com/people/Kangxi/6000000019155600599 Emperor Shêng-tsu], and when the other princes organized factions to contend for his place, [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] determine
2 CONC d to obtain the throne for himself. Since he achieved his aim and so was able to re-write the official records at will, little is known of his activities in the struggle for primacy during the last twenty years of his father's rei
2 CONC gn. But it is clear that, as a prince, he took pains to cultivate the friendship of such able courtiers as [https://www.geni.com/people/LKD/6000000010144669698 Lungkodo] and [https://www.geni.com/people/NGY/6000000010103693526 Nie
2 CONC n Kêng-yao] [qq.v.] and of those Bannermen in the companies assigned to him as his retainers. Judging from the irreconcilable attitude of his contending brothers, and from the harsh measures he used against them, it is clear that 
2 CONC their hatred of him was deep-rooted and was aggravated by numerous unrecorded incidents which made reconciliation impossible. Yet it is likely that if any of his opponents had become Emperor, [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng
2 CONC /6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] would have suffered similarly at his hands (see under Yin-ssû). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the second decade of the eighteenth century [https://www.geni.com/people/YT/6000000009822517461 Yin-t'i] [禵 q.v.], fourteenth son of [https://www.geni.com/people/Kangxi/6000000019155600599 Emperor Shêng-tsu] and a brother of [h
2 CONC ttps://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] by the same mother, was favored by the aged Emperor as Heir Apparent. In 1718 he was made commander-in-chief of the armies sent to the northwest to guard against i
2 CONC nvasion by the Eleuths and the Tanguts. Aware of his father's favor, [https://www.geni.com/people/YT/6000000009822517461 Yin-t'i], though far away in Kansu, kept in constant communication with his supporters, being evidently eager
2 CONC  for information about the situation at the capital. 
2 CONT
2 CONT However, late in 1722, [https://www.geni.com/people/Kangxi/6000000019155600599 Emperor Shêng-tsu] suddenly took ill and was kept in bed at his country villa, the Ch'ang-ch'un yüan (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/Kangxi/600
2 CONC 0000019155600599 Hsüan-yeh]). On December 16 [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] was sent to the Temple of Heaven to prepare himself ceremonially to represent the Emperor at the Winter Solistice Sa
2 CONC crifices which normally ended on December 22. But instead of completing these ceremonies, he was at the side of the Emperor when he died on December 20. According to the officially-prepared accounts, the Emperor declared to severa
2 CONC l of his sons and courtiers, before his death, that [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] should be his successor. Unofficial chroniclers aver, however, that it was [https://www.geni.com/people/YT/60
2 CONC 00000009822517461 Yin-t'i] and not [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] who was designated successor to the throne, and that the will was altered by [https://www.geni.com/people/LKD/6000000010144669
2 CONC 698 Lungkodo]. Recent studies in documents of the period seem to corroborate some of these assertions, all the more so because of discrepancies in [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên]'s own edicts r
2 CONC elating to the last days of his father. Some of the unofficial accounts actually assert that [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] murdered his father in order to take the other aspirants by surprise
2 CONC . However that may be, as soon as [https://www.geni.com/people/Kangxi/6000000019155600599 Emperor Shêng-tsu] died [https://www.geni.com/people/LKD/6000000010144669698 Lungkodo], as commandant of the Peking Gendarmerie, kept the ci
2 CONC ty under control. [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên], escorting his father's remains and guarded by soldiers with drawn swords, entered the city and was recognized as Emperor, without disturbance.
2 CONC  [https://www.geni.com/people/YT/6000000009822517461 Yin-t'i], the most powerful other aspirant, could not retaliate, since he was far away and under the surveillance of the two generals, [https://www.geni.com/people/NGY/600000001
2 CONC 0103693526 Nien Kêng-yao] and [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009834320242 Yen-hsin] [q.v.] who favored his opponent. 
2 CONT
2 CONT During the first years of his reign, [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] strenuously consolidated his power by putting his brothers under the surveillance of his friends and by appointing his own s
2 CONC upporters to key positions. Some opponents he eliminated by imprisonment or execution (see under Yin-ssû); he severely punished those who criticized him (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/ZST/6000000050052491882 Cha Ssû-t'ing
2 CONC ]); and even did away with some former supporters, perhaps to prevent the disclosure of secrets. Throughout his reign he was busy suppressing any intimations by his opponents that he may have usurped the throne (see under Tsêng Ch
2 CONC ing). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Of great significance to the dynasty was his policy of depriving the princes of their power to control the Bannermen who were allotted to them as retainers. When the Eight Banners were established (see under Nurhaci) only one belo
2 CONC nged to the Emperor; in theory, at least, the seven princes who each controlled a Banner had absolute power over the men in that Banner. But by 1651 three Banners had reverted to the control of the throne (see under Abahai, Dorgon
2 CONC  and Fu-lin) with the result that the power of some princes was considerably curtailed. There were princes, however, who still held absolute power over their retainers; and, as the new Emperor was keenly aware, they could exercise
2 CONC  it for their own advantage—as he had once done. Conscious of the danger of this system to the stability of the throne, [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] worked strenuously to reduce the power of
2 CONC  these princes (see under Yin-ssû, Yin-t'ang, and [https://www.geni.com/people/YT/6000000009822517461 Yin-t'i]) and make them more subservient to himself. In pursuance of these aims he compelled those princes who were young to att
2 CONC end a school inside the Palace, known as the Shang shu-fang 上書房, or Palace School for Princes. The tutors were select scholars who could be trusted to inculcate the virtues of obedience and lovalty and who would frown on heterodox
2 CONC  ideas. By such instruction the princes were kept in complete subservience to the throne for the remainder of the dynasty. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In view of the suspicions that surrounded his accession, it is natural that [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] should have been greatly concerned about his place in history. One of his first acts 
2 CONC as Emperor was to confiscate the manuscripts of the great encyclopaedia, ''Ku-chin t'u-shu chi-ch'êng'' (see under Ch'ên Mêng-lei), in order to deprive his opponent, Yin-chih [q.v.], of the name of having sponsored that monumental
2 CONC  project. He suppressed so many documents concerning his brother, [https://www.geni.com/people/YT/6000000009822517461 Yin-t'i] (see above), that little is now known about the latter's expedition to Lhasa in 1720 (see under [https:
2 CONC //www.geni.com/people/YT/6000000009822517461 Yin-t'i] and [https://www.geni.com/people/YX/6000000009834320242 Yen-hsin]). Knowing that some official records of the latter part of his father's reign were unfavorable to himself and 
2 CONC that some were favorable to his opponents, he decided to suppress or alter any records which he disliked. One of the revealing facts about the ''shih-lu'', or "veritable records", of his father's eventful reign is that they occupy
2 CONC  a smaller compass per year than the ''shih-lu'' of any other Emperor of the Ch'ing period. [The average number of volumes of shih-lu for each of the reign-periods is as follows: Shun-chih, 1.7 volumes per year; K'ang-hsi, 1.1; Yu
2 CONC ng-chêng, 3; Ch'ien-lung, 6.2; Chia-ch'ing, 4.4; Tao-kuang, 5; Hsien-fêng, 9; T'ung-chih, 10.7; Kuang-hsü, 3.21. The chief editor of the ''shih-lu'' for the K'ang-hsi period was [https://www.geni.com/people/ZTY/6000000075506967984
2 CONC  Chang T'ing-yü] [q.v.] who, by [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên]'s last will, was given the highest award ever granted by a Ch'ing Emperor to a civil official—namely, to have his name celebrated
2 CONC  in the Imperial Ancestral Hall along with several generals who had helped to found the dynasty. It would seem that, in the opinion of [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên], [https://www.geni.com/peo
2 CONC ple/ZTY/6000000075506967984 Chang]'s editorship of the ''shih-lu'' of the K'ang-hsi period was not less important than a military victory in support of the throne. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên]'s policy towards the Jesuits and other missionaries in China was largely influenced by the question of his accession to the throne. He disliked the missionaries 
2 CONC because some of them had taken the side of his opponents (see under Sunu and Yin-t'ang). Those who had official posts in Peking he tolerated, but deported many others who worked in the provinces. Yet in 1727 he received with due c
2 CONC ourtesy a Portuguese envoy who came to Peking to ask for more lenient treatment of the missionaries. When in 1730 a severe earthquake reduced many of the buildings in Peking to ruins, be contributed to the reparation of the church
2 CONC es. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] proved to be in able and conscientious ruler; he reformed the national finances; kept strict watch over officials: and tried to enforce the laws of the empire. H
2 CONC e forbade officials to form cliques, and his 朋黨論 ''P'êng-tang lun'', "Discourse on Parties and Cliques", published in 1725, was a warning on this matter. Because officials were often tempted to engage in corrupt practices, owing t
2 CONC o inadequate salaries, he introduced the system of ''yang-lien'' 養廉, or extra stipends for "the cultivation of incorruptibility". In the last years of his aged father's reign many officials had lapsed into corruption, but [https:/
2 CONC /www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên]'s enforcement of the law rejuvenated the government and laid the foundation for the splendors of the succeeding reign (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/Qianlong/60
2 CONC 00000009796722780 Hung-li]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Politically successful, [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] was less fortunate in military affairs. In 1723 and 1724 [https://www.geni.com/people/NGY/6000000010103693526 Nien Kêng-yao] and his aide
2 CONC , Yüeh Chung-ch'i [q.v.], suppressed an uprising of the Khoshotes of Kokonor and subjugated that region, but after [https://www.geni.com/people/NGY/6000000010103693526 Nien] was removed (1725) the border campaigns ceased. In Yunna
2 CONC n [https://www.geni.com/people/Ortai/6000000009825736768 O-êr-t'ai] [q.v.] tried to eliminate the hereditary rulers of the Miao tribes and for a time appeared successful (1728-31), but not long after he had left the region the Mia
2 CONC o again rebelled and all his efforts were nullified. The attempt of [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] to conquer the Eleuths suffered an even worse setback. As his father had done in the case of 
2 CONC [https://www.geni.com/people/Galdan/6000000040214066522 Galdan] [q.v.], he first made peace with Russia (1727, see under [https://www.geni.com/people/Tulisen/6000000083145578212 Tulišen]). But despite extensive preparations, the C
2 CONC hinese forces were almost annihilated (1731) at the hands of the Eleuths (see under Furdan). For a time he was uneasy about the effects of this defeat on the Mongols, but a victory over the Eleuths at Erdeni Tsu in 1732 (see under
2 CONC  [https://www.geni.com/people/CR/6000000013442875874 Tsereng]) gave him confidence to make peace with them without undue loss of prestige (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/AKD/6000000010764290467 A-k'o-tun]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT One result of this war was the establishment, in 1729, of the Chün-chi ch'u 軍機處, or Grand Council. Prior to this time the Grand Secretariat was the office from which memorials were transmitted, where they were preserved, and where
2 CONC  imperial edicts were drawn up. In the K'ang-hsi period some edicts were framed in the Imperial Study (see under Chang Ying). By tradition, each memorial or edict which passed through the Grand Secretariat had to have several tran
2 CONC scriptions. But the delays and disclosures which this system entailed were found to he dangerous, particularly in time of war. When [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] decided to make war on the El
2 CONC euths, he established the Grand Council to deal with military affairs speedily, and to guard state secrets with more care. His successors, even in time of peace, retained the Grand Council, entrusting it with most of the duties of
2 CONC  the Grand Secretariat and the Imperial Study whose work was then confined to the supervision of records in the archives or the drawing up of unimportant state papers. Obviously only men trusted by the Emperor were appointed to se
2 CONC rve as Grand Councilors and only those with exceptional abilities were selected as secretaries in the Grand Council. 
2 CONT
2 CONT An ambitious ruler, [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] tried to exercise control over the thoughts of his people. He re-issued his father's so-called Sacred Edict of sixteen moral maxims, adding l
2 CONC ong expositions of his own. This work, entitled ''Shêng-yü kuang-hsün'', and its vernacular version (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/Kangxi/6000000019155600599 Hsüan-yeh]), became a widely used textbook for the improvement 
2 CONC of manners. It was repeatedly supplemented by hortatory edicts designed to keep officials obedient and the common people submissive. [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] took advantage of the cases 
2 CONC of Tsêng Ching and Lü Liu-liang [qq.v.], not only to justify his succession but to vindicate the Manchu conquest of China. His condemnation of Lü was due in part to the latter's advocacy of a racial revolution. To justify Manchu r
2 CONC ule and his own policy in particular, he published the ''Ta-i chüeh-mi lu'' (see under Tsêng Ching) which for a time every licentiate in the empire was compelled to read. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Not satisfied with his temporal power, [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] assayed the role of a religious leader. In 1732 he transformed the Yung-ho Kung 雍和宮, the palace in which he had lived befo
2 CONC re becoming Emperor, into a Lama temple. Though he thus paid his respects to Lamaism, he was at heart a Buddhist of the Ch'an (Zen 禪) sect, and perhaps even had an ambition to unite Confucianism, Buddhism and Taoism into one relig
2 CONC ion. During his last years (1732?-35) he assembled a group of fourteen persons for the study of Ch'an Buddhism—a group which included, besides himself, five princes, three high officials, five Buddhist monks and a Taoist priest. I
2 CONC n 1732 he edited a collection of writings and sayings of thirteen Buddhists and two Taoists, entitled 御選語錄 ''Yü-hsuan yü-lu'', 19 ''chüan'', printed in 1733. In this collection he included his own views under the title 圓明居士語錄 ''Yü
2 CONC an-ming chü-shih yü-lu''. At the same time he seems to have established a press to reprint Buddhist works. In 1734 he reprinted the 宗鏡錄 ''Tsung-ching lu'', 100 ''chüan'', by the priest, Yen-shou 延壽 (904-975); and, early in 1735, m
2 CONC ade an outline of that work, entitled ''Tsung-ching ta-kang'' (大綱), 20 ''chüan''. He also made a start at reprinting the sutras, but by 1735 only twenty-seven of them were published under the collective title 佛經二十七種 ''Fo-ching êr-
2 CONC shih-ch'i chong''. He condensed twenty sutra into a work, entitled 經海一滴 ''Ch'ing-hai i-ti'', 6 ''chüan'', printed in 1735. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The religious efforts of [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] were not confined to promoting orthodox teachings. In 1733 he published a work, entitled 揀魔辨疑錄 ''Lien-mo pien-i lu'', 8 ''chüan'', in wh
2 CONC ich he attacked a school of Ch'an Buddhism as unorthodox. An abbot, named Yüan-wu 圓悟 (H. 密雲, 1566-1642), had a disciple named Fa-tsang 法藏 (H. 漢月, 1573-1635), who wrote a work, 五宗原 ''Wu-tsung yüan'', printed in 1628, in which he se
2 CONC t forth views that were distinctly unorthodox. Yuan-wu pointed out Fa-tsang's errors by correspondence, but a disciple of Fa-tsang, named Hung-jên 弘忍 (H. 潭吉), wrote a work, entitled Wu-tsung chiu (救), in which he defended the view
2 CONC s of Fa-tsang. Shortly after the last mentioned work was published, Yüan-wu wrote (1638) a long discourse, entitled 闢妄救略說 ''P'i wang-chiu lüeh-shuo'', 10 ''chüan'', in which he vigorously attacked Hung-jên's views as heterodox. [h
2 CONC ttps://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] denounced both Fa-tsang and Hung-jên; ordered their works to be burnt; and forced their proponents to renounce them. In the ''Lien-mo pien-i lu'' he cited a number
2 CONC  of passages from the writings of the two men, pointed out their alleged errors, and wrote a vehement denunciation of their views. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is said that [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] believed in the longevity theories of the Taoists and that he took various kinds of drugs, from whose effects he died. However that may be, it is
2 CONC  significant that all the Buddhists and Taoists were expelled from the Palace about the time of his death. Among the many legends concerning the manner of his death is one that he was murdered by the daughter of a man whom he had 
2 CONC executed. lit view of the many enemies he undoubtedly made, this supposition is not improbable. Nevertheless, official accounts state that he died peacefully in the Yüan-ming yüan (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/Qianlong/6
2 CONC 000000009796722780 Hung-li]) at the age of fifty-eight (''sui''). He was buried in the tomb known as T'ai-ling 泰陵, the first to be built in the Western Mausoleum (Hsi-ling 西陵) in the district west of Peiping. He was given the post
2 CONC humous name '''Hsien Huang-ti''' 憲皇帝 and the temple name '''Shih-tsung''' 世宗. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] had ten sons and four daughters, of whom four sons and one daughter lived to maturity. Fully aware of the danger of designating an Heir Apparent, he put the name
2 CONC  of his successor in a sealed box behind the tablet bearing the characters Chêng-ta kuang-ming 正大光明 in the hall Ch'ien-ch'ing Kung 乾清宮. The name, supposed to be known to no one, was to be revealed only after his death. In this way
2 CONC  he chose his fourth son, [https://www.geni.com/people/Qianlong/6000000009796722780 Hung-li] [q.v.]. As to his other sons, the third, Hung-shih 弘時 (1704-1727), led a wanton life and died young. He so offended his father that his n
2 CONC ame was struck from the ''Yü-tieh'' 玉牒, or Genealogy of the Imperial Family; and not until 1735, when [https://www.geni.com/people/Qianlong/6000000009796722780 Hung-li] ascended the throne, was it restored. [https://www.geni.com/p
2 CONC eople/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên]'s fifth son, Hung-chou 弘晝 (H. 旭日居士, Jan., 1712-1770), was made a prince of the first class with the designation Ho Ch'in-wang 和親王. He was canonized as Kung 恭 and left a literary collec
2 CONC tion known as 稽古齋全集 ''Chi-ku chai ch'üan-chi'', 8 ''chüan''. One of the wealthiest princes of his day, he took pleasure in assembling the paraphernalia necessary to his own funeral, and having the rites rehearsed before him while 
2 CONC he wined and dined. [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên]'s sixth son, Hung-yen 弘曕 (1733-1765), inherited the first class princedom left by his uncle, Yin-li (see under [https://www.geni.com/people/K
2 CONC angxi/6000000019155600599 Hsüan-yeh]), and became in 1738 the second Prince Kuo (果親王). In 1763, on the charge of greed and imprudent conduct, he was degraded to a prince of the third degree. Shortly before he died he was raised on
2 CONC e degree to a Chün-wang 郡王. He was canonized as Kung 恭. His great-grandson, I-hsiang 奕湘 (T. 楚江, d. 1881), inherited the rank of Prince of the fifth degree (1833) and served as Tartar General at Canton (1843-45), at Mukden (1845-47
2 CONC ), and elsewhere. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên]'s literary collection, entitled ''Shih-tsung yü-chih wên-chi'' (御製文集), 30 ''chüan'', was printed in 1738. His more important writings were primarily political do
2 CONC cuments, most of which he composed himself. He wrote comments and instructions on most of the memorials submitted by provincial officials. A collection of memorials bearing his comments, entitled 雍正硃批諭旨 ''Yung-chêng chu-p'i yü-chi
2 CONC h'' (often known as ''Chu-p'i yü-chih''), contains examples submitted by 223 officials arranged in 112 volumes in 18 cases. Some of these comments are much longer than the original memorials, showing the pains he took in national 
2 CONC affairs. The collection was printed in 1732 with additional materials printed in 1738. Some of his edicts concerning Bannermen and the Banner system were collected under three titles: ''Shang-yü Pa-ch'i'' 上諭八旗, 13 ''chüan''; ''Sha
2 CONC ng-yü Ch'i-wu i-fu'' 旗務議覆, 12 ''chüan''; and 諭行旗務奏議 ''Yü-hsing Ch'i-wu tsou-i'', 13 ''chüan''. His edicts issued through the Grand Secretariat, entitled ''Shang-yü nei-ko'' (內閣), 159 ''chüan'', concern national affairs. All the ed
2 CONC icts issued from 1722 to 1727 were printed in 1731; those issued in the years 1728-35 were edited and printed in 1741. There are two other collections of his edicts; one dealing with Buddhism, dated between the years 1733 and 1735
2 CONC ; and the other concerning right conduct and similar themes, issued in 1729. A classified selection of his edicts, entitled ''Shih-tsung shêng-hsün'' 聖訓, 36 ''chüan'', was printed in 1741. Some hitherto unpublished writings of his
2 CONC  have recently appeared in periodicals issued by the Palace Museum, Peiping. Some of these concern [https://www.geni.com/people/NGY/6000000010103693526 Nien Kêng-yao] and other officials whom he at first praised highly but later c
2 CONC ondemned to death or imprisonment. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Judging from his edicts, [https://www.geni.com/people/Yongzheng/6000000009796617943 Yin-chên] was a very able and ambitious man, but jealous. It is said that his spies swarmed in the empire and that almost every important action o
2 CONC f an official was reported to him. His reign has been branded as cruel and some of his officials as unjust (see under T'ien Wên-ching). Nevertheless, many of his acts were beneficial to the empire, or at least to the reigning hous
2 CONC e. His reorganization of the finances brought a higher income to the state; corruption was checked; power was centralized in the hands of the Emperor; and laws were enforced. All of these reforms contributed in some degree to the 
2 CONC splendors of the succeeding Ch'ien-lung period. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [ 1/9/la; 1/226/17a; ''Shih-tsung Hsien Huang-ti shih-lu''; ''Ch'ing Huang-shih ssû-p'u'' (see under Fu-lung-an); ''Ku-kung T'ien-pên-shu-k'u hsien-ts'un mu'' (see bibl. under Ch'ên Mêng-lei); ''Tung-hua lu'', Yung-chêng, passim; 
2 CONC Backhouse and Bland, ''Annals and Memoirs of the Court of Peking'' (1914), pp. 239-309; ''Lettres édifiantes et curieuses, Missions de la Chine'', pp. 330-697; 樞垣記略 ''Shu-yüan chi-lüeh''; ''Ch'ing-ch'u san ta i-an k'ao-shih'' (see
2 CONC  bibl. under Fu-lin); ''Pa-ch'i chih-tu k'ao-shih'' (see bibl. under Dorgon ), ] 
2 CONT
2 CONT FANG CHAO-YING
